# 1. Correct Real-Time Architecture

Instead of the UI loading the video, the **vision service publishes frames**.

```text
Video
  │
  ▼
Vision Service
  ├─ detections
  └─ frame stream
        │
        ▼
Redis
        │
        ▼
Tracking Service
        │
        ▼
UI
```

Now UI displays **the same frames used by detection**.

This guarantees **perfect overlay**.

---

# 2. Publish Frames From Vision Service

Update `vision_service/main.py`.

Import:

```python
import base64
import cv2
```

Before publishing the message, encode the frame.

```python
_, buffer = cv2.imencode(".jpg", frame)

frame_base64 = base64.b64encode(buffer).decode("utf-8")
```

Now modify the message.

```python
message = {
    "camera_id": camera_id,
    "frame_id": frame_id,
    "timestamp": time.time(),
    "frame": frame_base64,
    "detections": detections
}
```

So every event now includes the **actual frame**.

---

# 3. Tracking Service Passes Frame Forward

Update `tracking_service/main.py`.

Add frame to the output event.

```python
event = {
    "camera_id": message["camera_id"],
    "frame_id": message["frame_id"],
    "frame": message["frame"],
    "objects": objects
}
```

Now the **tracked_objects stream contains the frame**.

---

# 4. Update UI To Read Frame From Redis

Modify `ui/app.py`.

Add:

```python
import base64
import numpy as np
```

Decode the frame.

```python
frame_bytes = base64.b64decode(message["frame"])

frame_array = np.frombuffer(frame_bytes, dtype=np.uint8)

frame = cv2.imdecode(frame_array, cv2.IMREAD_COLOR)
```

Now draw tracks.

```python
frame = draw_tracks(frame, message["objects"])
```

Convert for Streamlit.

```python
frame = cv2.cvtColor(frame, cv2.COLOR_BGR2RGB)

frame_window.image(frame)
```

